// @emit-error ref-list field element type is not interned
// A generic application as the ELEMENT of an inline-object field's list type
// (`{ v: Pair<i32, string>[] }`). The emitter's inline-shape interner splits the
// field list at its top-level commas, and its depth alphabet was `{}` `[]` `()`
// with no `<>` — so the argument list's own comma cut the FIELD list, the second
// half (`string>[]`) had no `:`, and `internInlineShape` refused the whole shape.
// Nothing downstream knew why: the annotation reached the type section as a bare
// "ref valtype with no interned shape".
//
// With the brackets counted (`tyGtIsClose`, D-GENBRACKET) the shape interns with
// its one code-5 ref-list field, and the reject moves to the gap that is actually
// left: the element `Pair<i32, string>` is a generic APPLICATION, and the
// element-intern path (`internShapeFieldElems`) only descends a `{…}` leaf, so
// the list's element type is still un-interned. A named (non-generic) element
// alias — `type PairIS = { a: i32, b: string }; { v: PairIS[] }` — compiles and
// runs today, which is what separates this residual gap from the scanner defect
// above it.
//
// This is the ONLY program in the 1,282-file corpus or the 25,200-program fuzz
// A/B whose behaviour the `<>` repair changes: everywhere else a mis-split can
// only make a shape fail to intern (`ci <= 0` / an unclassifiable field code both
// set `ok = false`), never intern a wrong one, so the wrong answer is absorbed by
// a downstream reject.
type Pair<A, B> = { a: A, b: B }

const o: { v: Pair<i32, string>[] } = { v: [{ a: 1, b: "z" }] }
print(o.v[0].b)
